arXiv:2509.25414v1 [cs.LG] 29 Sep 2025</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Rethinking Parameter Sharing for LLM Fine-Tuning with Multiple LoRAs</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Hao Ban &amp; Kaiyi Ji
</span><span id="id1" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_correspondence ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Corresponding author.</span></span></span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Department of Computer Science and Engineering
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>University at Buffalo
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">{haoban,kaiyiji}@buffalo.edu</span></a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Large language models are often adapted using parameter-efficient techniques such as Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA), formulated as <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=W_{0}x+BAx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=W_{0}x+BAx</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is the pre-trained parameters and <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is the input to the adapted layer.
While multi-adapter extensions often employ multiple LoRAs, prior studies suggest that the inner <math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> matrices are highly similar during training and thus suitable for sharing. We revisit this phenomenon and find that this similarity is largely attributable to the identical initialization rather than shared knowledge, with <math id="abstract1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> playing a more critical role in knowledge encoding and transfer. Motivated by these insights, we propose <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ALoRA</span>, an asymmetric multi-LoRA design with multiple <math id="abstract1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> matrices and a single shared <math id="abstract1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> in multi-task fine-tuning, and <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Fed-ALoRA</span>, which shares <math id="abstract1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> across clients in federated fine-tuning under both homogeneous and heterogeneous settings, through a novel matrix decomposition strategy to accommodate heterogeneous ranks across clients. Experiments on commonsense reasoning, math reasoning, multi-task NLP dataset, and federated NLP dataset demonstrate that our methods achieve more balanced performance across tasks with comparable or superior average accuracy relative to existing multi-LoRA approaches. Codes are available at <a href="https://github.com/OptMN-Lab/ALoRA" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/OptMN-Lab/ALoRA</a>.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="p1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_top">
<tr id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:0.0pt;height:24.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"></span></td></tr>
</table>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large language models (LLMs) have achieved remarkable performance across diverse domains <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Achiam et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Comanici et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dubey et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, but the growing scale makes conventional full fine-tuning increasingly expensive. Parameter-efficient fine-tuning (PEFT) addresses this challenge by freezing the pre-trained model and updating only a small subset of parameters, improving efficiency while maintaining performance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Han et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Among PEFT methods, Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2022</a>)</cite> is particularly popular: it decomposes weight updates into trainable low-rank matrices <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>, which can be merged into the pre-trained model without extra inference latency.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Recent studies have shown that a single LoRA has limited capacity when handling diverse data distributions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib86" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cai et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. A natural extension is to use multiple LoRAs, where each module can specialize in different data modes such as tasks, domains, and distributed clients <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib82" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2024b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liao et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. In multi-task fine-tuning, adapters are required to handle task heterogeneity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, and in federated fine-tuning, they should account for client heterogeneity and personalization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bian et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. However, naively employing multiple LoRAs also increases computation and communication costs, which makes this approach less efficient.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To address this problem, recent methods explore parameter sharing across LoRA modules to improve parameter efficiency. HydraLoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tian et al., 2024</a>)</cite> observes that <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> matrices trained on different tasks exhibit very high similarity, and proposes a single shared <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> with multiple <math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>s for multi-task fine-tuning. FedSA-LoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2025</a>)</cite> reports similar findings in federated fine-tuning and transmits only <math id="S1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> matrices for server aggregation with reduced communication costs. These studies attribute the high similarity in <math id="S1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> matrices to the shared knowledge.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we revisit this similarity phenomenon and find that the similarity of <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> stems mainly from identical initialization rather than shared knowledge. Our analysis of learning dynamics reveals that <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> functions largely as a feature projector, while <math id="S1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> encodes the domain knowledge. A further exploration shows that sharing <math id="S1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> yields more effective knowledge transfer than sharing <math id="S1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> in both multi-task and federated fine-tuning. These insights motivate an interesting but underexplored question: <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">might sharing the module <math id="S1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>, rather than <math id="S1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, be more effective for parameter and knowledge sharing?</span> In this paper, we provide a positive answer, with our main contributions as follows.</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose <span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ALoRA</span>, a new asymmetric multi-LoRA architecture for multi-task fine-tuning. It employs multiple <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> matrices and a single shared <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> matrix, where the <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> matrices are dynamically routed by the inputs. This design enables each <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> to explore distinct feature subspaces while encouraging knowledge transfer through the shared <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose <span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Fed-ALoRA</span>, which communicates only <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> matrices rather than full LoRA parameters for aggregation on server. It supports both homogeneous and heterogeneous settings with the same and different ranks across clients, whereas existing parameter-sharing federated fine-tuning methods focus only on the homogeneous case. In the homogeneous setting, Fed-ALoRA updates all <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> matrices locally, and transmits and aggregates only <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> matrices on server side. In the heterogeneous setting, direct aggregation of <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is infeasible due to their distinct sizes, so we decompose <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(B_{1},B_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(B_{1},B_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> with appropriate sizes and introduce an auxiliary matrix for further dimension adjustment. Compared to full LoRA aggregation, Fed-ALoRA reduces communicated parameters by up to 50% and 75% in the homogeneous and heterogeneous settings, respectively, while maintaining performance.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct extensive experiments on intra-domain multi-task benchmarks such as commonsense reasoning and math reasoning, cross-domain multi-task NLP dataset, and federated NLP dataset to evaluate the effectiveness of our approaches. Across all datasets, our methods consistently deliver more balanced performance with comparable or superior accuracy compared to existing methods. In particular, <span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ALoRA</span> surpasses the sharing-<math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> approach <span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">HydraLoRA</span>, improving average ROUGE-1 by +0.68 with a <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta m\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta m\%</annotation></semantics></math> (which quantifies performance balance via mean drop from single-objective baselines) gain of -1.94. Similarly, <span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Fed-ALoRA</span> outperforms the sharing-<math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> approach <span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">FedSA-LoRA</span>, achieving gains of +1.26 (homogeneous) and +1.96 (heterogeneous) with <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta m\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta m\%</annotation></semantics></math> gains of -2.08 and -2.65, respectively. Compared with approaches that aggregate full LoRA parameters, our method attains comparable performance, smaller <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta m\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta m\%</annotation></semantics></math>, and substantially reduced communication cost by transmitting much fewer parameters.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Background</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Low-Rank Adaptation</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Pre-trained language models exhibit low intrinsic dimensionality when adapt to downstream tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Aghajanyan et al., 2021</a>)</cite>. LoRA leverages this property by approximating weight updates through low-rank decomposition. Particularly, for a pre-trained weight matrix <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{0}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{out}}\times d_{\text{in}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{0}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{out}}\times d_{\text{in}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the weight updates is defined as <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta W=BA" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta W=BA</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times d_{\text{in}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times d_{\text{in}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{out}}\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{out}}\times r}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the rank <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\ll min(d_{\text{in}},d_{\text{out}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≪</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\ll min(d_{\text{in}},d_{\text{out}})</annotation></semantics></math>. During training, only <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> matrices are trainable. Hence, given the input <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{in}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{in}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the forward pass is expressed as:
<math id="S2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=y_{0}+\Delta y=W_{0}x+BAx." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=y_{0}+\Delta y=W_{0}x+BAx.</annotation></semantics></math>
In practice, <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is typically initialized using Kaiming Uniform <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al., 2015</a>)</cite>, and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is initialized as zero to ensure <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta W=\mathbf{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟎</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta W=\mathbf{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Fine-Tuning with Multiple LoRAs</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Multiple LoRA-based methods extend vanilla LoRA with additional modules to improve adaptability across heterogeneous domains <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib99" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zi et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dettmers et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. In multi-task fine-tuning, they often use MoE designs where LoRAs act as dynamically routed experts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Luo et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, while in federated fine-tuning, they aim to balance personalization with shared knowledge aggregation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">Raje et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib96" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025c</a>)</cite>. A common idea among these multi-LoRA approaches is to share the same matrix <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, based on the observation that <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> matrices from LoRAs trained on different tasks or clients are often highly similar. For example,
HydraLoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tian et al., 2024</a>)</cite> employs a single <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> matrix and multiple <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> matrices to express the weight updates:
<math id="S2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta W=\sum_{i=1}^{n}w_{i}B_{i}A," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta W=\sum_{i=1}^{n}w_{i}B_{i}A,</annotation></semantics></math>
where <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> matrices, <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the gating score for each <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. The federated multi-LoRA approach
FedSA-LoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2025</a>)</cite> shares only the <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> matrices for server aggregation, after which the server broadcasts the aggregated <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> to all clients. The model update of client <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> is given by:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Delta W_{i}^{t}=B_{i}^{t}\bar{A^{t}},\quad\bar{A^{t}}=\text{Agg}(A_{1}^{t-1},\cdots,A_{n}^{t-1})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>Agg</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Delta W_{i}^{t}=B_{i}^{t}\bar{A^{t}},\quad\bar{A^{t}}=\text{Agg}(A_{1}^{t-1},\cdots,A_{n}^{t-1}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of clients, and <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is the current communication round, and <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Agg}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Agg</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Agg}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes an aggregation algorithm such as simple averaging.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Revisiting Parameter Sharing in Multi-LoRA Fine-tuning</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As noted earlier, a common strategy for parameter sharing is to reuse the same matrix <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> across multiple LoRA modules, with the goal of reducing the total number of parameters and enabling knowledge transfer. In this section, we systematically re-examine this approach through a series of controlled experiments. Full implementation details are provided in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Additional Details for Section ‣ Rethinking Parameter Sharing for LLM Fine-Tuning with Multiple LoRAs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Similarity in <math id="S3.SS1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> Stems from Same Initialization, Not Shared Knowledge</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A primary motivation for sharing <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> across LoRA modules is the observation that the matrices <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of different LoRAs often appear similar during training. However, upon closer examination, we find that this similarity largely arises from their common initialization rather than from the shared knowledge.
We fine-tune the LLaMA2-7B model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">Touvron et al., 2023</a>)</cite><span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
              
            <a href="https://huggingface.co/meta-llama/Llama-2-7b" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://huggingface.co/meta-llama/Llama-2-7b</a></span></span></span> separately on classification and summarization tasks from the Dolly-15K dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Conover et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, using either identical or different random seeds for <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> initialization (leading to different initializations for the matrices <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>), and compare the resulting LoRA modules using principal angle-based similarity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib98" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu &amp; Knyazev, 2013</a>)</cite>, where a value of <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> indicates complete similarity and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> indicates dissimilarity. The results are shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3.1 Similarity in 𝐴 Stems from Same Initialization, Not Shared Knowledge ‣ 3 Revisiting Parameter Sharing in Multi-LoRA Fine-tuning ‣ Rethinking Parameter Sharing for LLM Fine-Tuning with Multiple LoRAs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, and details of the similarity metric are discussed in Appendix <a href="#A2.SS1" title="B.1 Similarity Metric in LoRA Modules ‣ Appendix B Additional Details for Section ‣ Rethinking Parameter Sharing for LLM Fine-Tuning with Multiple LoRAs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Observation.</span> Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3.1 Similarity in 𝐴 Stems from Same Initialization, Not Shared Knowledge ‣ 3 Revisiting Parameter Sharing in Multi-LoRA Fine-tuning ‣ Rethinking Parameter Sharing for LLM Fine-Tuning with Multiple LoRAs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>(Left) shows that with the same initialization, <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> matrices are highly similar. In contrast, Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3.1 Similarity in 𝐴 Stems from Same Initialization, Not Shared Knowledge ‣ 3 Revisiting Parameter Sharing in Multi-LoRA Fine-tuning ‣ Rethinking Parameter Sharing for LLM Fine-Tuning with Multiple LoRAs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>(Middle, Right) shows that with different initializations, <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> matrices from either the same or different tasks exhibit little similarity, while <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> matrices display relatively higher similarity. These results suggest that the <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is highly sensitive to random seeds rather than necessarily capturing shared knowledge, whereas <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is less affected.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.25414v1/diff_task_same_init.svg" id="S3.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:181/136;" width="181" height="136"></object></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.25414v1/same_task_diff_init.svg" id="S3.F1.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:181/136;" width="181" height="136"></object></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.25414v1/diff_task_diff_init.svg" id="S3.F1.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:181/136;" width="181" height="136"></object></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Layer-wise similarity analysis between different LoRA modules. Left: two different tasks with the same random seed. Middle: the same task with different random seeds. Right: two different tasks with different random seeds. <math id="S3.F1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> matrices are similar only under the same initialization, whereas <math id="S3.F1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> exhibits relatively stable similarity across different tasks and seeds.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Dissecting Distinct Dynamics of <math id="S3.SS2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> During Training</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The above analysis motivates us to further investigate the learning dynamics of <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> during training by comparing their states before and after fine-tuning on the summarization task<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
              
              
              
            We use the checkpoints from the second and final steps, since <math id="footnote2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is initialized to zero at the beginning.</span></span></span>. Specifically, we examine the LoRA modules <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta W</annotation></semantics></math> (see Section <a href="#S2.SS2" title="2.2 Fine-Tuning with Multiple LoRAs ‣ 2 Background ‣ Rethinking Parameter Sharing for LLM Fine-Tuning with Multiple LoRAs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>), <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>.
Our experiments evaluate (i) the similarity of modules <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> (using the similarity metric in Section <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Similarity in 𝐴 Stems from Same Initialization, Not Shared Knowledge ‣ 3 Revisiting Parameter Sharing in Multi-LoRA Fine-tuning ‣ Rethinking Parameter Sharing for LLM Fine-Tuning with Multiple LoRAs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>) and (ii) the magnitude and directional variations of <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta W</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>. To formalize this<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
              
              
              
            We follow the same setup as in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. (2024)</a></cite>.</span></span></span>, any weight matrix <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{out}}\times d_{\text{in}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{out}}\times d_{\text{in}}}</annotation></semantics></math> can be decomposed into a magnitude and a direction component:
<math id="S3.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W=\|W\|_{c}\frac{W}{\|W\|_{c}}=mV," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mi>W</mi><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>c</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W=\|W\|_{c}\frac{W}{\|W\|_{c}}=mV,</annotation></semantics></math>
where <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\|\cdot\|_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em">∥</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\cdot\|_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the column-wise norm. Here, <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in\mathbb{R}^{1\times d_{\text{in}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in\mathbb{R}^{1\times d_{\text{in}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the magnitude vector, with <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> denoting the norm of the <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-th column of <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{out}}\times d_{\text{in}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{out}}\times d_{\text{in}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the direction matrix with unit-norm columns.
Given two matrices <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, their magnitude and direction discrepancies are defined as</p>
<table id="S3.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Delta M=\tfrac{1}{d_{\text{in}}}\sum_{j=1}^{d_{\text{in}}}|m_{1,j}-m_{2,j}|,\qquad\Delta D=\tfrac{1}{d_{\text{in}}}\sum_{j=1}^{d_{\text{in}}}\big(1-\cos(V_{1,j},V_{2,j})\big)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Delta M=\tfrac{1}{d_{\text{in}}}\sum_{j=1}^{d_{\text{in}}}|m_{1,j}-m_{2,j}|,\qquad\Delta D=\tfrac{1}{d_{\text{in}}}\sum_{j=1}^{d_{\text{in}}}\big(1-\cos(V_{1,j},V_{2,j})\big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.25414v1/same_client_diff_step.svg" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:181/136;" width="181" height="136"></object></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.25414v1/magnitude.svg" id="S3.F2.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:181/136;" width="181" height="136"></object></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.25414v1/direction.svg" id="S3.F2.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:181/136;" width="181" height="136"></object></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Comparison of LoRA modules before and after the fine-tuning. Left: similarity; Middle: magnitude change; Right: direction change. The module <math id="S3.F2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> remains largely unchanged from initialization, whereas the module <math id="S3.F2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> exhibits pronounced variation in both magnitude and direction. Overall, LoRA shows limited magnitude change, with nearly all directional change captured by <math id="S3.F2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Observation.</span> Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3.2 Dissecting Distinct Dynamics of 𝐴 and 𝐵 During Training ‣ 3 Revisiting Parameter Sharing in Multi-LoRA Fine-tuning ‣ Rethinking Parameter Sharing for LLM Fine-Tuning with Multiple LoRAs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>(Left) shows that <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> remains highly similar throughout training, undergoing only minimal changes, whereas <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> exhibits much larger differences, indicating substantial adaptation after fine-tuning. Figures <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3.2 Dissecting Distinct Dynamics of 𝐴 and 𝐵 During Training ‣ 3 Revisiting Parameter Sharing in Multi-LoRA Fine-tuning ‣ Rethinking Parameter Sharing for LLM Fine-Tuning with Multiple LoRAs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>(Middle, Right) further reveal that the variations in <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> are primarily in magnitude with little directional change, while <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> accounts for most of the direction change. These results suggest that <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> functions more as a fixed feature projector, whereas <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> aggregates and adapts these features to encode domain knowledge. This highlights the more dominant role of <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> in knowledge learning, raising an intriguing question: might sharing the module <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>, rather than <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, be more effective for parameter and knowledge sharing?</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Comparison Between Sharing <math id="S3.SS3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and Sharing <math id="S3.SS3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we address the question from Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Dissecting Distinct Dynamics of 𝐴 and 𝐵 During Training ‣ 3 Revisiting Parameter Sharing in Multi-LoRA Fine-tuning ‣ Rethinking Parameter Sharing for LLM Fine-Tuning with Multiple LoRAs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> by comparing the performance of sharing modules <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> under both multi-task and federated fine-tuning.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_floatright" style="width:50%;">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.25414v1/shared_A_vs_B_grad_magnitude.svg" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:135/101;" width="135" height="101"></object></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.25414v1/shared_A_vs_B_grad_conflict.svg" id="S3.F3.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:135/101;" width="135" height="101"></object></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>
Comparing sharing <math id="S3.F3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> versus <math id="S3.F3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> in multi-task fine-tuning.
Left: gradient magnitudes of <math id="S3.F3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.F3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>.
Right: number of gradient conflicts per layer.
Sharing <math id="S3.F3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> causes smaller gradient magnitudes and more frequent conflicts than sharing <math id="S3.F3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Gradient conflicts may lead to lazy learning for <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> in multi-task fine-tuning</span>.
Given an input <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{in}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{in}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and the gradient of the output <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{out}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{out}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the gradient of <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> in the sharing-<math id="S3.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> structure is
<math id="S3.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla A=\sum_{i=1}^{n}w_{i}(B_{i}^{\top}g)x^{\top}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla A=\sum_{i=1}^{n}w_{i}(B_{i}^{\top}g)x^{\top},</annotation></semantics></math>
where each term corresponds to a <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> expert. We record the magnitudes of <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla A</annotation></semantics></math> and compute the cosine similarity between gradient components, where negative similarity indicates a conflict that may hinder learning.
The same procedure is applied to the sharing-<math id="S3.SS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> structure. We then compare the two structures on the commonsense reasoning dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, tracking both the gradient magnitudes of shared parameters and the number of conflicts throughout training. The results are shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.3 Comparison Between Sharing 𝐴 and Sharing 𝐵 ‣ 3 Revisiting Parameter Sharing in Multi-LoRA Fine-tuning ‣ Rethinking Parameter Sharing for LLM Fine-Tuning with Multiple LoRAs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Observation.</span> Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.3 Comparison Between Sharing 𝐴 and Sharing 𝐵 ‣ 3 Revisiting Parameter Sharing in Multi-LoRA Fine-tuning ‣ Rethinking Parameter Sharing for LLM Fine-Tuning with Multiple LoRAs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>(Left) shows that the gradient magnitude of <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> in the sharing-<math id="S3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> structure is near zero, while the gradient of <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> in the sharing-<math id="S3.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> structure is much larger. Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.3 Comparison Between Sharing 𝐴 and Sharing 𝐵 ‣ 3 Revisiting Parameter Sharing in Multi-LoRA Fine-tuning ‣ Rethinking Parameter Sharing for LLM Fine-Tuning with Multiple LoRAs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>(Right) shows that sharing <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> also produces more gradient conflicts.
Thus, in the sharing-<math id="S3.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> structure, <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> learns very slowly possibly due to the more frequent conflicting updates. We refer to this phenomenon as “lazy learning”. Previous analysis in Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Dissecting Distinct Dynamics of 𝐴 and 𝐵 During Training ‣ 3 Revisiting Parameter Sharing in Multi-LoRA Fine-tuning ‣ Rethinking Parameter Sharing for LLM Fine-Tuning with Multiple LoRAs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> indicates that <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> functions as a feature projector. Hence, “lazy learning” may restrict the ability to explore diverse feature subspaces.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Knowledge transfer in federated fine-tuning</span>. Each client fine-tunes its own LoRA and transmits the shared parameters to the server, which aggregates and returns them (full details can be found in Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Fed-ALoRA for Federated Fine-Tuning ‣ 4 Proposed Methods ‣ Rethinking Parameter Sharing for LLM Fine-Tuning with Multiple LoRAs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>). This setup allows us to assess whether the shared parameters improve knowledge transfer by evaluating each client’s performance across all tasks. We compare the two structures across 8 clients, each assigned an NLP task from the FLAN dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib80" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, and use ROUGE-1 score  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin, 2004</a>)</cite> to measure performance, where a value of 0 means no overlap between model prediction and ground truth, and 100 indicates perfect word-level overlap.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Comparing sharing <math id="S3.T1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> versus <math id="S3.T1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> in federated fine-tuning. Sharing <math id="S3.T1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> consistently outperforms sharing <math id="S3.T1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> in both the homogeneous and heterogeneous settings.</figcaption>
<div id="S3.T1.2" class="ltx_logical-block">
          
