</figure>
<div id="S3.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Observation.</span> As shown in Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.3 Comparison Between Sharing 𝐴 and Sharing 𝐵 ‣ 3 Revisiting Parameter Sharing in Multi-LoRA Fine-tuning ‣ Rethinking Parameter Sharing for LLM Fine-Tuning with Multiple LoRAs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, in the homogeneous setting, sharing <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> outperforms sharing <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> by an average of 49.71%, with improvements ranging from 16.57% to 141.73%. In the heterogeneous setting, sharing <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> achieves an average improvement of 23.41%, with gains ranging from 12.49% to 41.38%. These results clearly indicate that sharing <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> better facilitates cross-client knowledge transfer than sharing <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Proposed Methods</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Motivated by the findings in Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Revisiting Parameter Sharing in Multi-LoRA Fine-tuning ‣ Rethinking Parameter Sharing for LLM Fine-Tuning with Multiple LoRAs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we replace <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> as the shared parameter and propose two new multi-LoRA fine-tuning methods: <span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ALoRA</span> (Asymmetric LoRA) for multi-task training, and <span id="S4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Fed-ALoRA</span> for both homogeneous and heterogeneous federated settings.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>ALoRA for Multi-Task Fine-Tuning</h3>

<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.25414v1/alora.svg" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:302/142;" width="302" height="142"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>ALoRA adopts multiple <math id="S4.F4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and a single <math id="S4.F4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> to explore diverse feature subspaces.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Multi-task fine-tuning typically adapts a pretrained LLM using data from multiple tasks. The goal is to improve generalization by learning from diverse inputs. The proposed ALoRA is illustrated in Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.1 ALoRA for Multi-Task Fine-Tuning ‣ 4 Proposed Methods ‣ Rethinking Parameter Sharing for LLM Fine-Tuning with Multiple LoRAs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. Given an input <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{in}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{in}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the forward pass is given by</p>
<table id="S4.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle y=y_{0}+\Delta y=W_{0}x+B\sum_{i=1}^{n}w_{i}A_{i}x," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle y=y_{0}+\Delta y=W_{0}x+B\sum_{i=1}^{n}w_{i}A_{i}x,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{0}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{out}}\times d_{\text{in}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{0}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{out}}\times d_{\text{in}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the pre-trained weight matrix, <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times d_{\text{in}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times d_{\text{in}}}</annotation></semantics></math> are the expert matrices, <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{out}}\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{out}}\times r}</annotation></semantics></math> is the shared aggregator, and the rank <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\ll min(d_{\text{in}},d_{\text{out}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≪</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\ll min(d_{\text{in}},d_{\text{out}})</annotation></semantics></math>. Each <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> projects the input into a distinct feature subspace, and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> fuses the learned features to produce the output. The expert weights <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w=(w_{1},\ldots,w_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w=(w_{1},\ldots,w_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> are obtained from an input-aware router, implemented as a linear gating function with parameters <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{g}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d_{\text{in}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{g}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d_{\text{in}}}</annotation></semantics></math>:
<math id="S4.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w=\text{softmax}(W_{g}x)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>softmax</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w=\text{softmax}(W_{g}x).</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">During the inference, the router computes input-dependent weights, and the weighted average of the adapters is dynamically merged into the pre-trained weights.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Fed-ALoRA for Federated Fine-Tuning</h3>

<figure id="S4.F5" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.25414v1/fed-alora-full.svg" id="S4.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/147;" width="548" height="147"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>Fed-ALoRA shares only <math id="S4.F5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> matrices for server aggregation. Left: Homogeneous setting (same rank), where the shared <math id="S4.F5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is directly transmitted. Right: Heterogeneous setting (different ranks), where the shared <math id="S4.F5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is decomposed into two matrices for heterogeneity. Compared to the standard full LoRA aggregation, the communication cost per client is reduced to <math id="S4.F5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(d_{\text{out}}r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(d_{\text{out}}r)</annotation></semantics></math> in the homogeneous setting and <math id="S4.F5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(d_{\text{out}}r_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(d_{\text{out}}r_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> in the heterogeneous setting if <math id="S4.F5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> is chosen appropriately.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Federated fine-tuning can be divided into two settings: (i) <em id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">homogeneous</em>, where all clients adopt the same configuration, and (ii) <em id="S4.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">heterogeneous</em>, where clients have varying capacities, introducing both computational and communication heterogeneity.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Homogeneous setting</span>. In this case, all <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> clients fine-tune their LoRA modules with the same rank. Each update takes the form <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta W_{i}=B_{i}A_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta W_{i}=B_{i}A_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times d_{\text{in}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times d_{\text{in}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{out}}\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{out}}\times r}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 4.2 Fed-ALoRA for Federated Fine-Tuning ‣ 4 Proposed Methods ‣ Rethinking Parameter Sharing for LLM Fine-Tuning with Multiple LoRAs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>(Left) illustrates the procedure of Fed-ALoRA for homogeneous setting, with the detailed steps for each communication round <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> shown below <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t\geq 1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t\geq 1)</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<ol id="S4.I1" class="ltx_enumerate ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">Step 1:</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Initialization.</span> If <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=1</annotation></semantics></math>, each client initializes <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> randomly and sets <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to zero. For <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are initialized with <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}^{t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}^{t-1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{0}^{t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{0}^{t-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">Step 2:</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Local training.</span> Each client performs LoRA fine-tuning on its local data, obtaining <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(A_{i}^{t},B_{i}^{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(A_{i}^{t},B_{i}^{t})</annotation></semantics></math> by optimizing <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}(W_{0}+B_{i}A_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(W_{0}+B_{i}A_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(A_{i},B_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(A_{i},B_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is the loss function. The client then uploads only <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> to the server for aggregation.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">Step 3:</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Aggregation.</span> The server aggregates the uploaded matrices using the operator <math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Agg}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Agg</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Agg}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">McMahan et al. (2017)</a></cite>, and obtains <math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{0}^{t}\leftarrow\text{Agg}(B_{1}^{t},\cdots,B_{n}^{t})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext>Agg</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{0}^{t}\leftarrow\text{Agg}(B_{1}^{t},\cdots,B_{n}^{t}).</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">Step 4:</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Broadcast.</span> The server then sends the global matrix <math id="S4.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{0}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>t</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{0}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> back to all clients.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S4.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Remark</em>. In the full-LoRA aggregation, each client communicates <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(d_{\text{in}}+d_{\text{out}})r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(d_{\text{in}}+d_{\text{out}})r</annotation></semantics></math> parameters per round. In contrast, Fed-ALoRA requires transmitting only <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, reducing the communication cost to <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\text{out}}r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\text{out}}r</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Heterogeneous setting</span>. In this case, clients may have different capacity constraints, resulting in parameterizations with diverse ranks <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, given by <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta W_{i}=B_{i}A_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta W_{i}=B_{i}A_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{r_{i}\times d_{\text{in}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{r_{i}\times d_{\text{in}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{out}}\times r_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{out}}\times r_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,n</annotation></semantics></math>. Because the ranks differ across clients, direct averaging of the <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> matrices is infeasible, and the aggregation strategy used in the homogeneous setting cannot be applied.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">To address this issue, we propose a novel decomposition strategy of the form:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Delta W_{i}=B_{i2}B_{i1}M_{i}A_{i}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Delta W_{i}=B_{i2}B_{i1}M_{i}A_{i},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p6.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{r_{i}\times d_{\text{in}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{r_{i}\times d_{\text{in}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{m}\times r_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{m}\times r_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i1}\in\mathbb{R}^{r_{i}\times d_{m}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i1}\in\mathbb{R}^{r_{i}\times d_{m}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i2}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{out}}\times r_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i2}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{out}}\times r_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The high-level idea is to decompose the matrix <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of the same dimension into two components, <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(B_{i1},B_{i2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(B_{i1},B_{i2})</annotation></semantics></math>, each of rank <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. We further introduce
<math id="S4.SS2.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> as an intermediate matrix to control the dimension <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>. In addition, every client maintains an accumulator <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i0}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{out}}\times d_{m}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i0}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{out}}\times d_{m}}</annotation></semantics></math> which stores the global updates it has received so far. The full procedure of round <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is illustrated in Figure <a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 4.2 Fed-ALoRA for Federated Fine-Tuning ‣ 4 Proposed Methods ‣ Rethinking Parameter Sharing for LLM Fine-Tuning with Multiple LoRAs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>(Right) and detailed below:</p>
<ol id="S4.I2" class="ltx_enumerate ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S4.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">Step 1:</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Initialization.</span> If <math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=1</annotation></semantics></math>, each client initializes <math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(A_{i},M_{i},B_{i1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(A_{i},M_{i},B_{i1})</annotation></semantics></math> randomly, and sets <math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(B_{i0},B_{i2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(B_{i0},B_{i2})</annotation></semantics></math> to zero. For <math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(A_{i},M_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(A_{i},M_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> is initialized with <math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(A_{i}^{t-1},M_{i}^{t-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(A_{i}^{t-1},M_{i}^{t-1})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i0}</annotation></semantics></math> is initialzed with <math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{0}^{t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{0}^{t-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i1}</annotation></semantics></math> is re-initialized randomly, and <math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i2}</annotation></semantics></math> is resets to zero.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">Step 2:</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Local training.</span> Each client performs LoRA fine-tuning on its local data, and obtains parameters <math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(A_{i}^{t},M_{i}^{t},B_{i1}^{t},B_{i2}^{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(A_{i}^{t},M_{i}^{t},B_{i1}^{t},B_{i2}^{t})</annotation></semantics></math> by optimizing <math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}(W_{0}+(B_{i0}+B_{i2}B_{i1})M_{i}A_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(W_{0}+(B_{i0}+B_{i2}B_{i1})M_{i}A_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to <math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(A_{i},M_{i},B_{i1},B_{i2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(A_{i},M_{i},B_{i1},B_{i2})</annotation></semantics></math>.
The client then uploads <math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(B_{i1}^{t},B_{i2}^{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(B_{i1}^{t},B_{i2}^{t})</annotation></semantics></math> to the server.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">Step 3:</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Aggregation.</span> The server reconstructs <math id="S4.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i}^{t}=B_{i2}^{t}B_{i1}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i}^{t}=B_{i2}^{t}B_{i1}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> for each client and then performs the aggregation <math id="S4.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{0}^{t}\leftarrow\text{Agg}(B_{1}^{t},\cdots,B_{n}^{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext>Agg</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{0}^{t}\leftarrow\text{Agg}(B_{1}^{t},\cdots,B_{n}^{t})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">Step 4:</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Broadcast.</span> The server then sends the global matrix <math id="S4.I2.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{0}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>t</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{0}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> back to all clients.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S4.SS2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Remark</em>. The previous parameter-sharing approach, FedSA-LoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, does not support the heterogeneous setting. Fed-ALoRA addresses this limitation by introducing the decomposition <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(B_{i1},B_{i2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(B_{i1},B_{i2})</annotation></semantics></math>, enabling efficient aggregation across clients with different capacities.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S4.SS2.p8.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Remark</em>. In the vanilla full-LoRA aggregation, each clients uploads <math id="S4.SS2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(d_{\text{in}}+d_{\text{out}})r_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(d_{\text{in}}+d_{\text{out}})r_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> parameters to the server. The server then extends all heterogeneous updates to the maximum rank <math id="S4.SS2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{\text{max}}=\max\{r_{1},\cdots,r_{n}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mtext>max</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\text{max}}=\max\{r_{1},\cdots,r_{n}\}</annotation></semantics></math> by padding with zeros, and broadcasts <math id="S4.SS2.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(d_{\text{in}}+d_{\text{out}})r_{\text{max}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mtext>max</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(d_{\text{in}}+d_{\text{out}})r_{\text{max}}</annotation></semantics></math> parameters back to every client.
In Fed-ALoRA, if <math id="S4.SS2.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> is chosen comparable to <math id="S4.SS2.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{\text{max}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mtext>max</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\text{max}}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS2.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{m}\ll\min(d_{\text{in}},d_{\text{out}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>≪</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{m}\ll\min(d_{\text{in}},d_{\text{out}})</annotation></semantics></math>, then client <math id="S4.SS2.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> maintains <math id="S4.SS2.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(d_{\text{in}}+d_{\text{out}}+2d_{m})r_{i}\approx(d_{\text{in}}+d_{\text{out}})r_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(d_{\text{in}}+d_{\text{out}}+2d_{m})r_{i}\approx(d_{\text{in}}+d_{\text{out}})r_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> trainable parameters. The communication cost is reduced to <math id="S4.SS2.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(d_{\text{out}}r_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(d_{\text{out}}r_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> from client to server, and <math id="S4.SS2.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(d_{\text{out}}r_{\text{max}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mtext>max</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(d_{\text{out}}r_{\text{max}})</annotation></semantics></math> from server to clients.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Experiments</h2>

<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Multi-Task Fine-Tuning</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We fine-tune the LLaMA3-8B model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dubey et al., 2024</a>)</cite><span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">4</span>
              
              
              
            <a href="https://huggingface.co/meta-llama/Meta-Llama-3-8B" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://huggingface.co/meta-llama/Meta-Llama-3-8B</a></span></span></span> on the <em id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">intra-domain</em> multi-task benchmark commonsense reasoning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, which contains 8 question answering (QA) datasets, each focusing on a different aspect of commonsense. We also fine-tune the LLaMA2-7B model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">Touvron et al., 2023</a>)</cite><span id="footnote5" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">5</span>
              
              
              
            <a href="https://huggingface.co/meta-llama/Llama-2-7b" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://huggingface.co/meta-llama/Llama-2-7b</a></span></span></span> on the <em id="S5.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">cross-domain</em> multi-task NLP dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Long et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, which mixes 8 different tasks such as QA, classification, and text generalization. These tasks are sampled from the FLAN dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib80" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. For each task, we first fine-tune LoRA on its own dataset and use the performance as the single-task baseline. We then compare the proposed ALoRA with several representative methods: the vanilla LoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, LoHa <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib88" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yeh et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, AdaLoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib93" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2023a</a>)</cite>, MoSLoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib81" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2024a</a>)</cite>, and HydraLoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tian et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. We also examine the math reasoning benchmark <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. Full details and further discussion are provided in Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Additional Experimental Details and Results ‣ Rethinking Parameter Sharing for LLM Fine-Tuning with Multiple LoRAs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate performance, we use the following metrics: (1) average accuracy for commonsense reasoning and average ROUGE-1 score for multi-task NLP dataset; and (2) <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta m\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta m\%</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Maninis et al., 2019</a>)</cite>, the average per-task performance change against the single-task baseline. <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta m\%=\frac{1}{K}\sum_{k=1}^{K}(-1)^{\delta_{k}}(M_{k}-M_{0})/M_{0}\times 100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>%</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>K</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta m\%=\frac{1}{K}\sum_{k=1}^{K}(-1)^{\delta_{k}}(M_{k}-M_{0})/M_{0}\times 100</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is the performance of <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-th task under the compared method, <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is the baseline performance. <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{k}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{k}=1</annotation></semantics></math> if higher values indicate better performance, otherwise <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{k}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{k}=0</annotation></semantics></math>. This metric evaluates how well performance is balanced across multiple tasks.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>Results on intra-domain multi-task commonsense reasoning benchmark. <math id="S5.T2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta m\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta m\%</annotation></semantics></math> measures performance balance across tasks. <math id="S5.T2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math> denotes that lower values are better. All methods use the same number of adapter parameters.</figcaption>
<div id="S5.T2.2" class="ltx_logical-block">
          
<div id="S5.T2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent ltx_align_center">
<div id="S5.T2.p1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:500.5pt;height:98pt;vertical-align:-46.5pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(0.0pt,0.0pt) scale(1,1) ;">
<p id="S5.T2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.T2.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text">
<span id="S5.T2.p1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<span id="S5.T2.p1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S5.T2.p1.1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt">Method</span>
<span id="S5.T2.p1.1.1.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">ARC-C</span>
<span id="S5.T2.p1.1.1.1.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">ARC-E</span>
<span id="S5.T2.p1.1.1.1.1.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">BoolQ</span>
<span id="S5.T2.p1.1.1.1.1.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">HellaS.</span>
<span id="S5.T2.p1.1.1.1.1.1.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">OBQA</span>
<span id="S5.T2.p1.1.1.1.1.1.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">PIQA</span>
