<span id="S5.T3.p1.1.1.1.1.8.11" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S5.T3.p1.1.1.1.1.8.11.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">-8.33</span></span></span>
</span></span></p>
</span></div>
</div>
        
</div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Federated Fine-Tuning</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We fine-tune the LLaMA2-7B model and evaluate on the federated dataset constructed by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Long et al. (2024)</a></cite>, which includes 8 NLP tasks sampled from FLAN dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib80" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, with each client assigned to one task. For each client, we first fine-tune LoRA on its own training dataset, and use the performance as the single-client baseline. We use ROUGE-1 as the evaluation metric.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In the homogeneous setting, we compare our Fed-ALoRA with: FedIT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib91" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, FedDPA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Long et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, and FedSA-LoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. In the heterogeneous setting, we compare Fed-ALoRA with: ZeroPadding, FLoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib79" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, and FedSA-LoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, which does not natively support heterogeneity but is adapted here using the decomposition proposed in our method. We also report the average number of parameters communicated per client in each round, including both uploads to the server and downloads from the server. In the homogeneous setting, all clients use rank 8. In the heterogeneous setting, the ranks are <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{64,64,32,32,16,16,8,8\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>64</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>64</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>32</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>32</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>16</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>16</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{64,64,32,32,16,16,8,8\}</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> set to 16. Full details are provided in the Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Additional Experimental Details and Results ‣ Rethinking Parameter Sharing for LLM Fine-Tuning with Multiple LoRAs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The results are presented in Table <a href="#S5.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 5.2 Federated Fine-Tuning ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Rethinking Parameter Sharing for LLM Fine-Tuning with Multiple LoRAs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> and Table <a href="#S5.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ 5.2 Federated Fine-Tuning ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Rethinking Parameter Sharing for LLM Fine-Tuning with Multiple LoRAs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. Fed-ALoRA achieves the most balanced performance while reducing communication cost by 50% compared to full-LoRA aggregation in homogeneous setting, and reducing by 75% in heterogeneous setting. Notably, the client with the word sense disambiguation (WSD) task performs poorly. However, Fed-ALoRA outperforms both the single-client baseline and full-LoRA aggregation in homogeneous setting and ranks second in heterogeneous setting. These results show that Fed-ALoRA effectively promotes knowledge sharing across clients.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T4" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 4: </span>Results for the <span id="S5.T4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">homogeneou</span>s federated setting. Params.(M) denotes the average number of parameters (in millions) transmitted per client in each round. ALoRA achieves the most balanced performance while reducing communication cost by 50% compared to full LoRA aggregation FedIT.</figcaption>
<div id="S5.T4.4" class="ltx_logical-block">
          
